WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.787
A pivot often requires aggregation.

00:00:02.787 --> 00:00:10.357
If only one source row maps to each output cell, the reshape can preserve that value directly.

00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:16.802
But if multiple source rows map to one cell, PostgreSQL needs an aggregate such as SUM, COUNT, MAX,

00:00:16.802 --> 00:00:22.332
or AVG. That aggregation changes the level of detail, so it must be chosen deliberately.

00:00:22.332 --> 00:00:31.137
Connect this idea to a BI dashboard turning monthly categories into presentation columns.

00:00:31.137 --> 00:00:36.852
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:36.852 --> 00:00:44.577
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:50.427
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:50.427 --> 00:00:56.320
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
